<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; Divine Revelation" cic="CCC 50-141" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in a group. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>1. Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What does the word &laquo;revelation&raquo; mean, and why is it important that God takes the initiative in revealing himself?</li>
<li>Name the three main stages of God's revelation to Israel that the class points out. Give one key event for each.</li>
<li>Why is Jesus Christ the full and definitive revelation of God, according to the Catechism (CCC 65)? What makes him different from all the prophets?</li>
<li>What are the three channels of transmission of revelation? Explain each one briefly and how they relate to one another.</li>
<li>What does the Catechism mean when it affirms that &laquo;no new public revelation is to be expected&raquo; (CCC 66)? Does this mean God stopped speaking?</li>
<li>Explain the relationship between Sacred Scripture and Sacred Tradition. Why can't we have one without the other (CCC 80-83)?</li>
<li>What is the Magisterium, and what is its role in interpreting God's Word (CCC 85-87, 100)?</li>
<li>What are the four senses of Scripture according to CCC 115-119? Name and briefly explain each one.</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>2. For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question honestly. There are no &laquo;right&raquo; answers to these personal questions.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. Have you ever had a &laquo;burning bush&raquo; experience in your life? A moment when you felt God was speaking to you in a special way.</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Describe briefly when it was and what you felt.</p>
<p class="question-block__p" style="margin-top:8px">___________ ___________ ___________ ___________ ___________</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. If God reveals himself gradually, what stage would you say you are living in right now?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Are you in a time of searching, of encounter, of crisis, of deepening? What makes you say that?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. Do you find it hard to believe that Jesus is God himself, or does it come naturally to you?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Reflect on which aspects of Jesus' person you find easier or harder to accept as God's revelation. Why do you think that is?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. What is your relationship with the Bible? Do you read it as God's Word or do you find it hard to see it that way?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">What would change in your daily life if you truly believed that God speaks to you through its pages &mdash; today, right now?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. Do you know someone who, like Saint Paul, had a radical conversion?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Or in your own life: have you experienced a deep change in how you see God? What triggered it?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">6. The Catechism says that &laquo;the Church has always venerated the Scriptures as she venerates the Lord's Body&raquo; (CCC 103). What does this comparison between the Bible and the Eucharist tell you?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Does it change how you might approach reading Scripture?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">7. Tradition is not a dead museum but a living stream. What traditions in the Church have helped you encounter God? (a particular prayer, a feast day, a family custom, a devotion...)</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Is there a tradition you'd like to rediscover or learn more about?</p>
</div>

</section>

